  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 242 PM CST Fri Nov 27 2026\n\nSprinkles to light rain have been on a downward trend through the \nfirst half of the afternoon hours. Latest radar only shows a few \nshowers across the area. Rainfall totals up to only about a \nhundredth or so has been reported in the light rain. High \ntemperatures have warmed to the the upper 30s, 40s, and a few 50s. \nTemperatures will cool back below freezing through the rest of the \nevening and overnight hours. Should see any wet surface dry out \nbefore this evening, especially as some sunshine breaks out. \nHowever, if any wet spots remain on untreated surfaces into this \nevening, they could refreeze and create some slick spots. At the \nsame time, westerly winds will remain elevated through the evening, \nresulting in wind chills near to below freezing. Low temperatures \nlook to bottom out in the low to mid 30s.\n\nAnother shortwave trough remains on track to eject into the Plains \non Saturday. This wave continues to show stretching deformation \nalong the 700mb thermal gradient, leading to frontogenesis (FGEN) in \nthis layer. The dendritic growth zone (DGZ) will also be saturated \nas well, with decent omega (upward motion) in the DGZ as well. \nLatest trends have continued to suggest a more southerly track \namongst the models. That said, the ensembles continue to show \nmoderate to high probabilities (50-90% chance) for rainfall totals \nto exceed a hundredth of an inch. These probabilities lower to \nalmost a 0% chance for a quarter of an inch. However, the REFS is \nthe only ensemble suggesting a further north solution, with \nlocations along and south of Highway-20 having about a 50% \nchance for rainfall totals to exceed a quarter of an inch. Given\nthis ensembles higher resolution, decided that it would be \nsuffice to keep the NBM's similar QPF in place. Should \nconfidence grow in a southerly track taking place, then QPF \namounts may be adjusted. Rain will slide through areas south of \nI-90 Saturday afternoon and evening before this rain exits the \narea to the southeast.\n\nHowever, this is not the last chance for precipitation. With cold \nair advection (CAA) in place on the backside of the departing \nsystem, low level lapse rates will remain steepened through the \novernight hours. These steepened low level lapse rates have the \npotential to yield cape values above 20 J/kg. Though these \nprobabilities are on the low side with only a 15-30% chance. \nHowever, this looks to be enough to produce rain and snow showers \nthrough the night. Have bumped up PoPs to about 15-20% for precip. \nThese probabilities will be refined over the next day as details \nbecome clearer.\n\nSunday through the middle of next week continues to look dry with \nnear to above seasonable highs as zonal flow takes over aloft. Highs \non Sunday look to remain largely in the 30s before highs warm to the \n40s to potentially 50F by Wednesday. \n\nMedium range guidance continues to show a stronger upper level wave \npassing through the southern CONUS towards the end of next week. \nMedium range guidance shows increasing variance in how the this \nstronger wave will evolve. The ensembles show a 30-60% chance for \nrainfall totals to exceed a tenth of an inch late Thursday through \nFriday. Will continue to keep an eye on how this wave evolves over \nthe next week. Aside from rain chances, highs look to remain in the \n40s for the end of next week.",
